  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nLate evening water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis reveal strong ridging is in place atop the Ark-La-Tex \nregion, with a potent cut-off low over the northwestern CONUS.\nAt the surface, a lee cyclone remains in place across eastern \nCO and adjacent areas, which has supported south-southwesterly \nwinds across the central plains the past couple days. Friday \nwill be no different as winds become sustained in the 15-25 mph \nrange gusting to 35 mph during the late morning through early \nevening hours. These downsloping winds along with strong solar \ninsolation will contribute to afternoon highs in the mid/upper \n90s once again. HREF members imply the majority of southwest KS \nwill remain dry Friday afternoon and evening under the influence\nof the strong upper ridge, however a few stray \nshowers/thunderstorms are possible favoring our northwest zones.\nOvernight, elevated southerly winds will limit Saturday morning\nlows to the mid 60s to mid 70s.\n\nDaytime Saturday, short range ensembles agree the aforementioned \npotent upper trough will open up and advance eastward to the \nnorthern Rockies, and this will coincide with a slight weakening \nof the ridge over the southern plains. Despite the subtle \nheight falls and a cold front impinging on our area, pre-frontal\ndownslope flow and plenty of sun will support one final day of \nafternoon highs in the 90s. This boundary may serve as the focus\nfor thunderstorm development Saturday afternoon/evening \nfavoring roughly the southeast half of our area, however HREF \nmembers exhibit plenty of disagreement on several factors.\n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nagree the upper trough will continue to track along the \nnorthern CONUS, further dampening the ridge in the process. In \nthe wake of a strong cold fropa late Saturday night/early Sunday\nmorning, and under lower 500-mb heights and westerly flow, \nsouthwest KS will enjoy its first prolonged stretch of fall-like\ntemperatures this season, as afternoon highs stay in the mid \n70s to mid 80s range through at least Thursday. Several \nopportunities for precipitation will also exist, most notably \nwith the frontal passage early Sunday morning when NBM has \nwidespread pops in the chance (25-54%), likely (55-74%), and \ndefinite (75-100%) categories.",
